{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's reasoning violates the §11.3 standard because it conflates redundancy with efficiency rather than impossibility. The §11.3 adjudication-ritual requires demonstrating that the flag is sustained by showing the verdict-shape is impossible to satisfy — not merely inefficient. By conceding that the joint requirement 'filters correctly' and is 'not impossible but merely inefficient,' the target actually refutes the very basis for sustaining the flag. A sustained §11.3 flag demands that the two criteria be mutually contradictory (impossible to jointly satisfy), but the target explicitly admits they are compatible ('not impossible'), which means the flag should not be sustained. The target thus fails the audit standard by sustaining a flag on grounds (inefficiency) that the standard does not recognize as valid.","counterexample":null,"premise":"The §11.3 adjudication-ritual sustains a flag only when the verdict-shape's criteria are impossible to jointly satisfy, not when they are merely redundant or inefficient.","premise_evidence":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}